Tomecast's patch to audio.cpp v0.9.4: an opt-in, faster flow sampler for Ming-3.0-tts.

This is Tomecast's own change, not upstream's (https://github.com/0xShug0/audio.cpp).
.github/scripts/build-audiocpp.sh applies it with `git apply` to both engine builds (CPU and
Vulkan), and the build fails if it doesn't apply cleanly.

What it does
  Ming-3.0-tts spends about 70% of its time in its flow-matching head. Upstream runs that as
  10 Euler steps per audio patch on a fixed, sway-warped time table (MingFlowRuntime::sample
  in src/framework/modules/speech_decoders/ming_flow_runtime.cpp). Two environment variables,
  read once per server process when it first voices with Ming, change the steps:

    AUDIOCPP_MING_FLOW_STEPS=N           solver steps, 1 to 10 (default 10)
    AUDIOCPP_MING_FLOW_SCHEDULE=default  the official table; with N < 10, its points round(10k/N)
                               =uniform  k/N, no warp
                               =sway     k/N, then the official warp t' = 1 - cos(pi/2 t)

  The solver (Euler), the guidance, the SDE term and every bf16 rounding step stay upstream's.

Defaults
  Unset or empty, both give upstream's sampler unchanged: the same 10 steps on the same time
  table, with the same arithmetic, so the audio is byte-identical to an unpatched build. The
  loop body is upstream's, untouched; the local experiment this patch was cut from was checked
  byte for byte on 20 of 20 chunks. An invalid value prints one warning and is ignored. Only
  Ming-3.0-tts uses this code.

Measured (2026-11-10)
  RTX PRO 1000 Blackwell Laptop (8 GB), a CUDA build for sm_120, one server, guidance 2.0,
  voice UK_George, seed 42, 60 chunks of Alice's Adventures in Wonderland, chapter I. "Words
  lost" compares a Parakeet TDT 0.6B v3 transcript with the text.

    Setting                      x real time   Words lost
    upstream (10 steps)          4.16          0.9% (11 of 1,166)
    STEPS=7                      5.33          1.5% (17 of 1,166)
    STEPS=5 SCHEDULE=uniform     6.54          1.7% (20 of 1,166)   <- recommended

  5 uniform steps run about 1.55x faster with nearly the same quality: every frequency band
  stays within 0.5 dB of 10 steps, with the same length and no new tones, and on a separate
  20-chunk run it lost exactly the words upstream did (1.8% each). At 5 steps the other
  schedules lose more words: sway 4.1%, default 3.9% (20 chunks).
  6 uniform steps (5.9x) is the fallback if 5 proves too rough. Only CUDA was measured.

Cut from the local experiment this came from: midpoint and Heun solvers (worse than Euler for
the same number of network passes), and skipping the unguided pass at guidance 0 (6% faster,
but guidance 0 sounds worse).

License: Apache 2.0, the same as audio.cpp. The changed lines are marked "Tomecast patch" in
the code, as section 4(b) of the license asks of modified files.

The build checks that the server it made contains these strings, so the patch is compiled in:
Binary-contains: AUDIOCPP_MING_FLOW_STEPS AUDIOCPP_MING_FLOW_SCHEDULE

